{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' established neighborhoods are beautiful — mature trees lining streets, deep lots with decades-old landscaping, homes with character newer subdivisions can't match. But all those trees create a constant stream of debris ending up in your gutters.</p>

<p>The <a href="/recent-projects/">neighborhoods around Athens State University, near the downtown courthouse square, and throughout central Athens</a> are hit hardest. Century-old oaks drop massive leaves every fall. Maples shed thousands of seeds in spring. Pecans scatter shells and debris year-round. By the time you've cleaned up from one season, the next one's already filling your gutters.</p>

<p>North Alabama gets 55+ inches of rain annually. When gutters clog, that water doesn't disappear — it overflows, runs down your siding, and pools around your foundation. Limestone County's clay soil doesn't absorb water quickly, which means pooling persists for days after heavy rain, slowly working toward your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>Many Athens homeowners fall into a dangerous pattern: they know gutters need cleaning, but life gets busy. Weeks turn into months. By the time they get around to it, gutters are packed solid with decomposed organic sludge and water damage has already started.</p>

<p><strong>The math:</strong> Foundation repair in Athens runs $10,000-30,000 depending on severity. Annual gutter cleaning runs $300-400 total. We've seen homeowners skip cleanings to save money, then spend five figures fixing foundation cracks. Don't learn this lesson the expensive way.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Our gutter cleaning is comprehensive — not the quick blow-and-go some services offer.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1: Hand removal.</strong> We pull all debris from gutter troughs by hand — leaves, seeds, roof grit, decomposed organic matter. We don't blow debris onto your lawn or push it toward downspouts (a common shortcut that causes clogs downstream). We bag it and haul it away.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2: Downspout flush.</strong> Every downspout gets flushed with water until it runs clear. This is critical. Downspout clogs are the #1 thing homeowners miss during DIY cleaning. Debris backs up in the downspout, water can't exit, and gutters overflow even though they look clean from above.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3: Inspection.</strong> While we're up there, we check for: loose or missing hangers, seam separations, small leaks, rust spots, and fascia showing early damage. We document problems and let you know what we find — no scare tactics, just honest assessment.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4: Cleanup.</strong> When we're done, your property looks better than when we arrived. No debris on the lawn, no mess on the driveway.</p>

<p><strong>What we won't do:</strong> Tell you everything is "fine" if it's not. We see problems constantly that previous cleaners ignored. You'll get an honest picture of what's happening up there.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've cleaned gutters across <a href="/recent-projects/">Athens and Limestone County</a> for over 20 years. From historic homes near the courthouse square to mid-century neighborhoods around Athens State, we've handled every type of debris problem and gutter configuration this area produces.</p>

<p>Headquartered 25 minutes away in Huntsville, we serve Athens regularly with responsive scheduling. When you call Blue River Gutters, you're talking to people who understand North Alabama — our trees, our clay soil, our rainfall patterns, and the specific challenges Athens homes face.</p>

<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> If you hire someone for gutter cleaning and they don't flush your downspouts, you're not getting complete service. Debris compacts into partial clogs that restrict flow — and you can't see them from above. Ask specifically. Some companies skip this step because it takes extra time. We don't.</p>

<p><strong>Our recommendation:</strong> For most Athens homes, schedule cleanings in late November (after leaves drop) and late April (after spring pollen/seeds). If you have significant pecan coverage, add one in August. Three cleanings a year keeps everything flowing. Or consider gutter guards if you're tired of the ongoing expense.</p>
{% endset %}
